[{:db/id "q-141236/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "3c35eb6b-9317-58c6-a1a2-d041b088d934" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141236/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "6265302e-71ae-51ef-a8a3-8bd45bb7403a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141236/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "baec33aa-250a-52ad-a175-1ef23d7ad061" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141236/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c5f9b004-85c4-5f0d-9409-c6c087b84e0f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141236/selection/b0d571b4f8c9a519d4151da0e7137238f1308365223597beae87aa81fb8d20bd" :answer/id #uuid "f9ad1e53-2956-5abb-88b0-13bfdbad64c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141236/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "67771a47-1176-5217-a18f-dbb5da86a9c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141236/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-141236/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-141236/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-141236/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-141236/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-141236/selection/b0d571b4f8c9a519d4151da0e7137238f1308365223597beae87aa81fb8d20bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141236" :question/id #uuid "a0455dfc-71d9-5697-8db9-62e19eeb0c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-141236" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-2)÷(2x)$\n- $(7x+1)\\cdot (2{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1)÷({x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{6}-2}{2x} & =\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{2x}-\\frac{2}{2x} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-141236/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141236"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141245/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "6a7137a5-b877-553e-944f-d7526dbb3abb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141245/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "0f54b6a4-085c-5f85-866e-c1ea1e60bb87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141245/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "83ab3ba6-3a6d-51e4-9109-32ac534d11c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141245/selection/b0d571b4f8c9a519d4151da0e7137238f1308365223597beae87aa81fb8d20bd" :answer/id #uuid "fbc056f9-1fea-57f8-89ee-1d238636584d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141245/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "eda44ca7-4f9c-5583-ae36-2030ee31bde4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141245/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "120e9f86-ba52-5322-aa73-b0b46dbc2ea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141245/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-141245/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-141245/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-141245/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-141245/selection/b0d571b4f8c9a519d4151da0e7137238f1308365223597beae87aa81fb8d20bd" "q-141245/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141245" :question/id #uuid "8bf388ce-2466-5600-a83d-d8315936fc03" :question/math-academy-id "q-141245" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(3{x}^{3}-2x)÷({x}^{3})$\n- $(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3})$\n- $(2x-1)\\cdot ({x}^{3}+6x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & =5{x}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141245/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141245"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141240/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "042d5b69-3606-5780-b44c-539f0ee4a926" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141240/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "b39c808d-a60c-5dda-9e0b-aab6b0634696" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141240/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "0693c42d-6d57-5b8d-8a90-c9dc6e2febc4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141240/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "777ad2ff-bff1-5c4f-ac5b-9608e6509b55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141240/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "e16708c3-dece-5d8f-b9a9-d44aa1ae1d91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141240/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f0fbd1d9-1ff7-5fc6-825d-2de23b2c5361" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141240/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-141240/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-141240/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-141240/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-141240/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-141240/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141240" :question/id #uuid "28e6d8df-ffe4-5059-9c8f-21069da9bee7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141240" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-x)+(3{x}^{5})$\n- $({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x({x}^{2}-4x)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}-4x)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141240/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141240"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141234/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "b59fdc75-fd8c-5168-a0c6-2aa8dae53efc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141234/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "9cc11679-aabd-5a6b-81fa-9bc49d982f32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141234/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "a1a4e71d-cde9-5e2d-b051-5e10b92f54b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141234/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "63824682-6cd1-5d8e-b03d-af409b2c83d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141234/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "5f3c881e-b7a4-594b-aa34-899a8c060404" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141234/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d48d013-a62b-561f-be1d-f3b4767c0f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141234/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-141234/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-141234/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-141234/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-141234/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-141234/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141234" :question/id #uuid "43473837-68b6-5be4-987f-f091e37ac03d" :question/math-academy-id "q-141234" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$\n- $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-141234/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141234"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-141203"] :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $({x}^{2}+4x)-(-5{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $(2-{x}^{3})÷({x}^{2})$\n- $(2x-{x}^{3})\\cdot ({x}^{2}-x+1)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-269884/field-1/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer/id #uuid "d456c80b-092e-5927-a47e-2a20cb348114" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-269884/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "428a0aa6-bef8-5caa-90f8-4090efd9f261" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-269884/field-1/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer-field/choices ["q-269884/field-1/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-269884" :question/id #uuid "f88ab8dd-7297-5a21-afd7-67cb8387d601" :question/math-academy-id "q-269884" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $11$ and $9$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $11,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{11}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{11}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{11}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{11+9}={x}^{20}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $20.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-269884/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-269884"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-277901/field-1/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" :answer/id #uuid "6919a49e-e9a8-5a0f-9b22-adce85177cf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277901/field-1/ad7a3824e2b90d316aedd9968e2fdff13c67ee5110546b7a43eeb384aa267fd0" :answer/id #uuid "a0b77b80-4cde-5a46-a4d4-b73c8cd6179d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "112" :db/ensure [:answer/