an ${x}^{7},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-46423/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-46423"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-277910/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7ee5e5e7-98da-51da-95d5-5ed6d742e102" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277910/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "73a2ff56-d924-5308-a3d9-d68a2285860a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-277910/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-277910/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-277910" :question/id #uuid "62208363-aadd-5a8b-bb77-eb9afb9030cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-277910" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$, the **smallest possible** degree of $P-Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P=4{x}^{3}$ and $Q=4{x}^{3}-10.$ Then $P-Q=10,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-277910/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-277910"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-51690/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ff0f3521-188e-534e-a080-ab5bbfff4d69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-51690/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "3dce5b35-f1df-585a-ba39-72729a85473d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-51690/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "941528ec-baeb-5cf8-818d-f4748219ebdc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-51690/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "eee7dea7-eb3f-591f-922d-c93a7b4293fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-51690/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "641553a4-68f4-59a2-9675-7d126a1fdb7e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-51690/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b97a78ab-ed0a-5203-a2a4-f3508b6bad51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-51690/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-51690/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-51690/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-51690/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-51690/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-51690/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-51690" :question/id #uuid "cff243a3-fd98-53b6-858c-867e5a03ee26" :question/math-academy-id "q-51690" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $3{x}^{3}-P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $3{x}^{3}+P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P=3{x}^{3}-1.$ Then $3{x}^{3}-P(x)=3{x}^{3}-(3{x}^{3}-1)=1,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P=-3{x}^{3}.$ Then $3{x}^{3}+P(x)=3{x}^{3}+(-3{x}^{3})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree term in $P$ must contain ${x}^{3}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{3}={x}^{3+3}={x}^{6},$ meaning that $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ has degree $6.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-51690/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-51690"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-111385/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2260fcd5-3374-554d-abee-7b008df8f628" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111385/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "b6f6e3b6-2354-58ad-bb9c-fb06da5e613e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111385/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "7de7608d-fb39-5c91-8866-00bc20a344fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111385/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "1fcf11d6-572f-57e3-af69-334511c72244" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111385/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e398e850-417f-580c-88b4-456922585e7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111385/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c81186fb-2a3d-5ae5-865f-8b1dc7654013" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-111385/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-111385/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-111385/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-111385/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-111385/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-111385/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-111385" :question/id #uuid "d1492386-6d0c-51d6-9c6a-e6f64d3cb61e" :question/math-academy-id "q-111385" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $4,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{4},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ that could cancel the ${x}^{4}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P-Q$  still contains ${x}^{4}.$  Therefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must always be $4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the cases where $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is a polynomial, we need that the degree of $P$ should be greater or equal to the degree of $Q,$ which is not the case.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-111385/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-111385"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-46457"] :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $6,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{6},$ respectively. In $P+Q,$ the leading term still contains an ${x}^{6}.$ There is no ${x}^{6}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{6}$ term in $Q.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $6.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{6}.$ Then $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-111390/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8c79a1f6-1faa-5d64-8906-f514e2654892" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111390/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "712e9ae8-b93b-533d-9fba-c670a1ce55fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111390/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "c62eec66-2f1f-574f-a9ef-a8145c8d3d91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111390/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "771d660b-ae72-53ff-aa82-80c0a3243a6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111390/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "4522e59e-0963-5636-a5b9-67b82da1f4b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111390/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a814c045-d35a-5bdb-aa7a-ae5c09ccd3d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-111390/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-111390/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-111390/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-111390/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-111390/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-111390/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-111390" :question/id #uuid "fc10624a-1d97-52d8-b614-80cd282f5aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-111390" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-111390/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-111390"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-1316"] :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\n- $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\n- $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3})÷(5x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3}}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x(x-2{x}^{2})}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5x}(x-2{x}^{2})}{\\cancel{5x}} \\\\  & =x-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9981"] :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an $x^8.$\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q,$ the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing $x^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-1317"] :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an $x^3.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3.$ There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-1318"] :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n\n- $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n\n- $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10},$ meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5.$ Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5.$ Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
